= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ be the top-down optical depths at $P_1$ and $P_2$, and let $\mu$ be the outward direction cosine. The <formal solution of the radiative transfer equation> gives
$$
\boxed{
I_\nu(0,\mu)
=B_\nu(T_1)(1-e^{-\tau_1/\mu})
+B_\nu(T_2)(e^{-\tau_1/\mu}-e^{-\tau_2/\mu})
+I_{\nu,0}e^{-\tau_2/\mu}}.
$$
For a semi-infinite lower layer in <local thermodynamic equilibrium>, $I_{\nu,0}=B_\nu(T_3)$.

Define
$$
E_3(x)=\int_0^1\mu e^{-x/\mu}\,d\mu.
$$
The emergent planetary surface flux is
$$
F_{p,\nu}=2\pi\left[
B_1\left(\frac12-E_3(\tau_1)\right)
+B_2(E_3(\tau_1)-E_3(\tau_2))
+B_3E_3(\tau_2)\right].
$$
Hence the band-centre planet-star ratio is
$$
\boxed{
\frac{F_p}{F_*}
=\left(\frac{R_p}{R_*}\right)^2
\frac{2[B_1(1/2-E_3(\tau_1))
+B_2(E_3(\tau_1)-E_3(\tau_2))+B_3E_3(\tau_2)]}
{B_\nu(T_*)}}.
$$
If $T_1=T_2=T_3=T_p$, the weights telescope to $1/2$, so $F_{p,\nu}=\pi B_\nu(T_p)$ and the expression reduces to the blackbody <thermal eclipse depth>, about $72\,\mathrm{ppm}$ for $T_p=600\,\mathrm K$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $20\,\mu\mathrm m$,
$$
E_3(0.1)=0.4163,
\qquad
E_3(1)=0.1097.
$$
The normalized flux weights of the $400$, $600$, and $800\,\mathrm K$ layers are therefore
$$
2(1/2-E_3(0.1))=0.1674,
$$
$$
2(E_3(0.1)-E_3(1))=0.6132,
\qquad
2E_3(1)=0.2194.
$$
Using the <Planck law> at the band centre and $R_p/R_*=0.0354$ gives
$$
\boxed{F_p/F_*\simeq7.45\times10^{-5}simeq74.5\,\mathrm{ppm}}.
$$
This is only slightly larger than the $71.7\,\mathrm{ppm}$ isothermal $600\,\mathrm K$ value because the cool upper-layer suppression and hot deep-layer enhancement nearly cancel in this broad weighting.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

